  "text": "Three questions remain, and each has a short answer. Sensitive or robust? A sensitive metric moves reliably when the treatment works, so it can detect small effects. A robust one is hard to game and isn't thrown by outliers. Revenue per user is long-tailed: here, capping it at the ninety-ninth percentile cuts its variance by forty-eight percent. Stratified randomization splits users into groups first, like country or device, then randomizes inside each one. With six heavy users among twenty, a coin per user puts five or six of them in one arm about one time in five. Stratifying puts three in each. And an A A test gives both groups the same experience, to test the platform itself. The page's code runs a thousand of them, and five percent come out significant, as they should. Far more than that means a bug. A test designed this carefully can still go wrong once it's live: the split comes back uneven, the effect fades, or ten metrics get read at once. Next: experiments in practice.",
